  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure well off the Mid-Atlantic coast will \nmove even farther away Thursday, however some coastal impacts and \nhazardous marine conditions continue.\n\nLow pressure located well off the Mid-Atlantic coast will strengthen \nthrough tonight as it gradually turns more to the northeast. This \nnortheast or east-northeast motion then continues on Thursday. \nMeanwhile, strong surface high pressure centered across New England \nwill shift into the Canadian Maritimes tonight. A tightened pressure \ngradient remains between these two systems through tonight. \n\nSome impacts remain only for our coastal and marine areas. The multi-\nday north-northeast wind has resulted in 10+ foot waves just \noffshore. These big waves breaking onto the beaches will result in \nsome additional beach erosion especially during times of high tide. \nPeak wind gusts in the 30-40 mph range into this evening for areas \nalong the coast, and 20-30 mph inland. The winds will diminish late \nthis afternoon and especially tonight particularly away from the \nimmediate coast. Some mid to mostly high clouds into tonight will \noccur especially closer to the coast. As the winds diminish tonight, \nit will be a chilly night, however frost is not expected.\n\nFor Thursday into Friday, the ocean storm will move farther out to \nsea as an incoming system from the northwest kicks it out. This will \nreduce the marine and coastal impacts, while keeping our weather dry \nand providing a fresh shot of chilly air. There will still be a \nnotable north-northwest breeze Thursday as the pressure gradient \nremains tightened due to another incoming strong surface high from \nCanada. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tide cycles persist due to a storm off the \ncoast with water levels peaking with this evening's high tide. \nWidespread minor coastal flooding is expected from Ocean County NJ \nsouthward through Delaware.\n\nAs a storm continues to linger off the coast, elevated tide cycles \npersist with water levels peaking this evening. Expect fairly \nwidespread but minor flooding from Ocean County NJ southward through \nDelaware including the Delaware shores of Delaware Bay. In some of \nthe back bays, moderate coastal flooding is still possible this \nevening however the chances for this have lowered some as recent \ntide cycles have generally not been quite as high as forecast. \nBeyond this evening, the storm will gradually be moving to the \nnortheast away from the region for the end of the week. Another \nround of minor coastal flooding is possible Thursday evening though \nat this time it's looking to be more spotty in nature. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for the \nMaryland eastern shore. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Frost impacts possible Friday night and Saturday \nnight where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast on Friday,\na potentially strong mid level ridge in the Plains to Midwest \nshifts eastward some into the weekend. This will support strong \nsurface high pressure initially centered near the Great Lakes and \nportions of eastern Canada, which then shifts eastward into the \nNortheast and Mid-Atlantic Friday and over the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead the winds \nwill decouple. This will set the stage for efficient radiational \ncooling both Friday night and Saturday night. The forecast lows are \nin the 30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and outside of \nthe urban centers) with a frost potential. It is possible that if \nthe radiational cooling ends up being stronger, then some areas \ncould get to freezing or a little below. Overall though, frost \nimpacts are possible where the growing season remains active.",
